  "name": "Nürburgring",
  "tagline": "The German GP layout: a tight arena, a long loop, and a chicane before the line",
  "difficulty": "Hard",
  "notes": [
    "Traced from the Formula 1 circuit map of the Nurburgring GP-Strecke -- ninety control points following the curves rather than only the numbered corners -- and then scaled until the sampled centreline measured 5.148 km, the published length. Shape from the map; length exact by construction.",
    "The footprint comes out about 12% larger than the real circuit's, and the reason is the spline rather than the trace. A uniform cubic B-spline does not INTERPOLATE its control points, it passes inside them, so the sampled centreline is consistently shorter than the polyline through the same points -- 4652 m against 5299 m here. Adding points does not close that gap: it converges on the smoothed curve, not on the polyline. Matching the published length therefore means scaling up until the smoothed curve is long enough.",
    "Going from thirty points to ninety was still worth it. It did not add length -- the polyline only grew 4% -- which is itself the finding: the first trace was not cutting corners, the assumed real footprint was simply wrong. What it bought was shape, and 5% off the scale needed.",
    "The corner numbers on the map run 1 to 15 in the direction of travel and the control points follow them: the Mercedes Arena loop straight after turn 1, the long run down to the far hairpin, back up the inside, across the top, and through the chicane onto the start straight.",
    "Elevation is invented. The map carries none, so this is one gentle wave over the lap with a dip at the far hairpin, kept small enough not to fight the terrain envelope."
  ],
